<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">I've Been Working on the Railroad</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">first published <a href="/years/1894">1894</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;I've Been Working on the Railroad&quot; is an American folk song. Its first published version appeared as &quot;Levee <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>&quot; in Carmina Princetonia, a book of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princeton-university">Princeton University</a> songs, in 1894, and its earliest known recording was made by the Shannon Quartet, released by Victor Records in 1923.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Levee Song of 1894 is written in minstrel dialect. One of its verses is very close to the modern song, about working on the railroad all the livelong day and the captain calling Dinah to blow her horn, but it opens with an introduction for a soloist and quartet that is no longer sung, and its second verse, which uses a racial slur, is very different. The melody of the opening line may have been inspired by the very similar start of the cello solo in Franz von Suppé's Poet and Peasant overture of 1846.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The modern song joins several parts. After the railroad verses comes the Dinah, won't you blow refrain, which according to the liner notes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pete-seeger">Pete Seeger</a>'s Children's Concert at Town Hall (1963) was a more modern addition contributed by college students. The section that begins Someone's in the kitchen with Dinah, with a noticeably different melody, is an older song absorbed into this one: it was published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in the 1830s or the following decade as Old Joe, or Somebody in the House with Dinah, with music credited to J.H. Cave.</p><p>The tune of that section may have been adapted from Goodnight, Ladies, written as Farewell Ladies in 1847 by E.P. Christy. Dinah, the name of an abducted woman in the biblical story of Genesis, was a generic name for an enslaved woman and, by extension, for any woman of African-American descent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Other verses have circulated. One sung after the fiddly-i-o lines has someone making love to Dinah, since the old banjo can no longer be heard. The Family Car Songbook, researched and edited by Tam Mossman, prints further stanzas about driving spikes on a trestle, living as a hobo in the boxcars and one day owning the railroad.</p><p>The tune also carries The Eyes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, the spirit song of the University of Texas at Austin and the University of Texas at El Paso. Its words were written in 1904, and students, staff and alumni sing it at Longhorn sports games and other events.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “I've Been Working on the Railroad” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>I've Been Working on the Railroad</b>&quot; is an American folk song.</blockquote><a href="/songs/ive-been-working-on-the-railroad">I've Been Working on the Railroad</a></li></ul></section>
